.docname {Digital Twin}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Twin | The stored shape of a building: floors, rooms, sizes | Northfield Library, 3 floors |
    | Upload | One request to register a building, tracked until done | Status `ready` |
    | Worker | A loop inside the service that builds twins from a queue | Picks the next upload |
    | Placement | Where one sensor or router sits in the building | Router on floor 1, by the stairs |
    | Track | One phone followed from tick to tick | Moves from hall to room 2 |
    | Port | What the core needs from outside, said in its own words | "store this building" |
    | Adapter | The piece that does it with a real technology | MongoDB |

Realises the [Building Management](../domain-model/domain-buildings.qd) domain. Rust, MongoDB. Code-level page: [Digital Twin Service](../services/digital-twin.qd).

The source of truth for the shape of every building. Other services add live data on top of it.

---

## Who talks to it

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        C["client, agent"] --> Tw["digital-twin"]
        O["notification, telemetry, socket"] -- "who owns it?" --> Tw
        Tw == "registration, room counts" ==> T["telemetry"]
        T == "registration done, Wi-Fi ticks" ==> Tw
        Tw -. "device dots" .-> S["socket"]

Thin: a request. Thick: Kafka. Dotted: Redis.

It also calls three services directly:

| To | Sends | When |
|---|---|---|
| `telemetry` | Room limits | After an edit |
| `dashboard` | Starting columns | After registration, best effort |
| `notification` | "Upload failed" | When a registration fails |

| Job | In plain words |
|---|---|
| Buildings | Register, read, list, change owner organizations, send again to `telemetry` |
| Placements | Where each sensor and router sits |
| Wi-Fi estimate | Where phones probably are, room by room |
| Ownership lookup | Answers "which organizations own building X?" for the other services |

---

## Inside

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        subgraph In["Driving adapters"]
            H["HTTP"]
            W["Worker"]
            KC["Registration answers"]
            WC["Wi-Fi ticks"]
        end
        subgraph Core["Core"]
            UC["Use cases"]
            E["Entities and rules"]
            P["Ports"]
        end
        subgraph Out["Driven adapters"]
            M["MongoDB: buildings, uploads, placements"]
            KP["Kafka out"]
            R["Redis out"]
            O["HTTP to other services"]
        end
        H --> UC
        W --> UC
        KC --> UC
        WC --> UC
        UC --> E
        UC --> P
        P -.-> M
        P -.-> KP
        P -.-> R
        P -.-> O

- **Entities** hold what is true of a building whatever the storage, like "a room has a size above zero".
- **Use cases** are the actions, like "register" or "update", plus the ports they need.
- **Access rules run in the core.** "May Ada edit this building?" is a rule, and Cedar needs no network.
- **Arrows point inward.** Only the start-up code knows the store is MongoDB. Tests swap in fakes and need no database.
- A test fails the build if a layer imports the wrong way.

---

## Registering a building

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        participant A as Ada
        participant Tw as digital-twin
        participant T as telemetry
        participant N as notification
        A->>Tw: upload the building
        Note over Tw: check it, then save the upload
        Tw-->>A: accepted, with a handle
        Note over Tw: the worker picks it up and builds the twin
        Tw->>T: registration requested
        Note over T: store rooms, set limits
        T->>Tw: registration completed
        Note over Tw: upload is ready or failed
        Tw-->>N: failed? tell the owners

| Step | Why this way |
|---|---|
| Check before answering | A building the service would refuse is never accepted |
| Answer before building | Building is slow, and can fail for reasons Ada cannot fix |
| Ada polls the handle | She sees `ready` or `failed`, never silence |
| Announce on Kafka, do not call | `telemetry` can be down; the request waits for it |
| Ready only on the answer | Sending the request is not success. `telemetry` saying "done" is |

- **Three entries, one use case.** HTTP, the worker and the Kafka answer all go through the same registration logic, so the three cannot drift apart.
- **Twice is the same as once.** A message can come twice. Building again ends in the same one building.
- **Best effort extras.** Starting columns in `dashboard` and room limits are sent after; a failure is logged, not fatal.

---

## Estimating phones from Wi-Fi

Each tick says which routers hear which phone, and how loud.

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        Tick["Wi-Fi tick"] --> Grid["Score every spot on the floor plan"]
        Grid --> Prior["Weigh by where the phone was before"]
        Prior --> Spot["Most likely spot and room"]
        Spot --> Dots["Dots, no ids"]
        Spot --> Counts["Phones per room"]

1. The floor is split into small cells. For each cell, the service knows how loud each router should sound there, walls included.
2. Each phone gets the cell that best matches what the routers heard.
3. A phone cannot jump faster than a person walks, so its last place narrows the choice.
4. A phone unseen for a few ticks is dropped. One missed poll does not end it.

**Example.** Router A hears a phone loud, router B faint. The cells near A and far from B score best. Last tick the phone was in room 2, next door, so room 2 wins over the hall beyond it.

- A tick older than three polls is skipped: it shows where people were, not where they are.
- Nothing is stored. Dots go to `socket`, room counts back to `telemetry`.

---

## Choices

| Choice | Why |
|---|---|
| The service makes building ids | The id never depends on what a client or a database chose |
| Owners come from the pass | No call to identity services per request |
| Shape treated as fixed | Only names and owners change; the live data lives elsewhere |
| Same size checks on every write | A bad room is refused with a clear error, never stored |

---

## Gaps

- **Code vs domain: rooms cannot be edited.** The domain says staff edit rooms. The code fixes them at upload.
- **Code vs domain: starting Focus is a direct call** to `dashboard`, best effort, not a reaction to "Building Ready". If it fails, the building gets no live feed until someone saves its columns.
- **The request rate limit lives in memory.** Two copies of the service would each allow the full rate.
